msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:137
msgid "Resize or maximize the window to suit your workspace. Preview and Split File remember your window size when you save the project."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:140
msgid "The colored bar beneath the ruler shows sections already loaded for playback. If playback is choppy, try a smaller window or :ref:`optimize the file <optimize_ref>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:143
msgid "Preview File shows playback and scrubbing controls only. Preview Clip and Split File add range editing below the shared preview. The source ruler and filmstrip show the full recording, with the selected range highlighted. Drag either grip to adjust the range and move the playhead with it. Drag on the ruler above the filmstrip to scrub without changing the range. Edit the Start and End timecodes for precise boundaries, or click the adjacent clock button to use the current playhead position (:kbd:`I` / :kbd:`O`). End is the last included frame; a one-frame range is valid. Duration shows the length of the selected range and can be edited to move End while keeping Start fixed. Duration is limited to at least one frame and at most the remaining source length. The buttons beside Play jump to Start and End."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:155
msgid "When editing a timeline clip, :guilabel:`Update Clip` updates only that clip. Its position stays fixed and its duration changes; neighboring clips do not move. Locked or retimed clips can be previewed, but range application is disabled. :guilabel:`Reset` reloads the saved range. Each update is one undo step."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:160
msgid "Split File provides Start, End, Duration, and an optional Name beside :guilabel:`Create`. Each click creates one Project Files entry and leaves the window open to create another. Pressing :kbd:`Enter` in Name or outside the time fields saves and closes the editor. In Start, End, or Duration, Enter commits the value and keeps the editor open. Tab moves through Start, End, Duration, and Name; Shift+Tab reverses the order. Each creation is one undo step. Opening an existing split lets you update its range and name without changing existing timeline clips. :guilabel:`Update` saves the split and closes its editor, refreshing the file and dependent clip thumbnails. :guilabel:`Update Clip` saves and closes clip preview. Use the window close control or :kbd:`Esc` to close without applying changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:173
msgid "Drag a filmstrip handle to trim, drag inside the selection to move it, or drag outside it to draw a new range. Click to seek, or drag on the ruler to scrub without changing the range."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:177
msgid "If a modified range changes elsewhere in the project, discard its local changes and reload it before saving. The dialog remains attached to its original source or timeline clip when the main-window selection changes."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:185
msgid "Optimized preview files are lower-resolution copies of your source video files (sometimes referred to as proxy files), used to improve editing responsiveness without changing the original media. They are especially helpful with large formats such as 4K, high frame rate, or high bitrate footage."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:190
msgid "Automatic optimization"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:191
msgid "When OpenShot creates a video's first thumbnail, it records how long the first frame takes to produce. This uses work already needed to display the video in Project Files; there is no separate decoding test. Videos whose thumbnail frame takes longer than the :guilabel:`Auto-Optimize Threshold` may benefit from smaller preview copies."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:196
msgid "With the default :guilabel:`Ask` preference, a banner appears when OpenShot finds a qualifying video in a project that does not yet have a saved choice:"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:199
msgid "**Optimize videos for smoother playback?** Originals and export quality stay unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:201
msgid "Click :guilabel:`Optimize Automatically` to enable automatic optimization for the current project. The banner disappears immediately, and qualifying videos are added to the optimization queue. Videos you import later also join the queue when they qualify, even if another video is already being optimized. You are not asked again for each file."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:206
msgid "You can continue editing while optimization runs. There is no progress banner; progress appears on the files in Project Files instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:209
msgid "Click :guilabel:`Not Now` or close the banner to decline. OpenShot remembers your choice when you save the project, so reopening it or importing more videos does not repeat the suggestion. To change your choice, check or uncheck :menuselection:`File --> Auto-Optimize` or press :kbd:`Ctrl+Alt+O` (:kbd:`Command+Alt+O` on macOS). Customize this shortcut in :menuselection:`Edit --> Preferences --> Keyboard`. Turning it off stops new automatic work; jobs already started can be canceled through the file's :guilabel:`Optimize` submenu. Existing optimized copies remain available."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:217
msgid "In :menuselection:`Edit --> Preferences`, open the :guilabel:`Optimize` tab (see :ref:`preferences_optimize_ref`):"
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:220
msgid ":guilabel:`Auto-Optimize`: :guilabel:`Ask` shows the banner and is the default. :guilabel:`Always` optimizes qualifying videos without a banner. :guilabel:`Never` disables suggestions and automatic processing. These defaults apply to projects without a saved choice; a project's own setting takes precedence."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:224
msgid ":guilabel:`Auto-Optimize Threshold`: defaults to **200 ms**. Lower values include more videos; higher values include fewer. This measures producing the first thumbnail frame, not opening the file, saving the thumbnail, or waiting in a queue."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:227
msgid ":guilabel:`Optimized Preview Resolution` and :guilabel:`Concurrent Optimize Jobs` control the preview copies and how many videos can be processed at once."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:230
msgid "Thumbnail timing is saved with each file in the project. OpenShot reuses a valid timing when you reopen the project on the same computer. A changed source file or a different computer needs a new measurement. When opening older projects, OpenShot refreshes missing timings in the background, one small thumbnail at a time, checking videos used on the timeline first. New checks pause during playback. Slow videos trigger the same banner; accepting it also checks and queues the remaining videos. Enabling :guilabel:`File → Auto-Optimize` starts these checks too. Choosing :guilabel:`Never` or dismissing the banner stops further checks for that project. Save the project to keep its timings for next time. You can always optimize videos manually."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:240
msgid "The threshold is a guide, not a guarantee of playback performance. Other applications can affect timing, and effects or overlapping clips can slow playback independently of the source video. Optimized copies use additional disk space in the project's assets folder; original files and export quality stay unchanged. Canceled or failed automatic jobs are not automatically retried during the current project session."
msgstr ""

#: ../doc/files.rst:246
msgid "Closing OpenShot cancels queued and running optimization jobs. Active jobs finish their current processing step before closing, and unfinished preview files are removed."
msgstr ""

